A||N||3||glava2876||Front brake question||||1||
Z||000000||glava2876||07-28-2001||11:27 PM||||Well it has taken a lot of time and work, but the Fiero finally came off the jack stands after a 12 year rest. It is running and idling great -I WANT TO THANK EVERYONE FOR THEIR HELP AND PATIENCE!!.Remaining problems are as follow; 1) replaced front and rear brakes, but the front both seem to be hanging up -this happened when bleeding. I drove the car a short distance, but they got hot and drag pretty bad. Any idea what is wrong, I've never had this happen before. 2) The paint finish is a pretty dull white, but in good shape otherwise. Is this a clear coat paint job or can I just use a fine rubbing compound? 3) The AC won't cool, but does seem to engage OK.Guess I'll convert it. Thanks again,Glen||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||07-28-2001||11:33 PM||||You replaced the brakes? not the calipers? sounds like you need to replace the calipers or the rubber brake lines.<P>it's also posible the MC isn't returning to full rest. That can hold the brakes on. This could be caused by the MC or the power booster.<P>Let me guess.... you "pedal bled" the brakes? In that case the MC is a very likely culprit.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||glava2876||07-29-2001||11:35 AM||||Theogre, You are correct -I peddle bled the brakes. I had the front and rear rotors turned. I replaced the pads and the REAR brake line.The fluid on the rear brake side of the MC did empty -but not the front. The rear brakes seem OK -no dragging, but I didn't check them on jack stands to see if they are working. The car only has 37,000 miles, but sat for 12 years.If it is a problem at the MC, what is the remedy? Thanks, Glen  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-29-2001||03:25 PM||||<A HREF="http://lycos.howstuffworks.com/brake.htm" TARGET=_blank>Go here</A> and read the MC section of the article.... Carefully watch how the Secondary MC piston works. Pedal bleeding can hang that piston up with crap that is stuck in the MC. Repair means replacing the MC. (Click the red arrow to run the animation. Macromedia Flash is required.)<P>If you let the rear tank run dry durring bleeding you will likely have a very unpleasant time getting the MC blead. (reasons in my brake service article...) It won't hang the fronts but you will have a mush pedal.<P>Hopefully you didn't warp a rotor trying to drive it like that....<P>In you're case,,,, I'd replace both front brake lines and the MC. I would not trust them after being parked that long. Rebuild the front calipers unless there is piston damage. If the pistons are damaged buy rebuilt calipers. Remember the front brakes do most of the work. You absolutly do not want any of these parts to be marginal.<P>Buy a couple quarts of Valvoline's Syntec Synthetic brake fluid. (About $5.00 a quart and very hard to beat for the money.) completely flush the front brake lines before installing the new soft lines and rebuilt calipers. Flush more fluid out the rears as well. Try to drain the rears before flushing. If you try any of the rear draining ideas in my cave let me know how they worked and exactly what you did. Those are ideas I've played with but haven't had a chance to test. ||||reg||1
